+{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "39faadfc-108f-4032-afe9-c39356389017", "team_id": "contest2026_403_anpai", "github_login": "ma-abc123", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 1037, "ts": "2026-08-27T16:12:40.544Z", "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "tokens_in": 406, "tokens_out": 0, "role": "assistant", "thinking": "The user says the screen didn't light up this time, but they don't want me to fix it yet — just find the cause. Let me think about what could cause intermittent black screen.\n\nLooking at the log from the previous successful boot:\n```\nLCD device ready after 400 ms\nLVGL init (LCD only)\nLCD open success\nTouch initialized (delayed)\n```\n\nThe screen lit up that time. But this time it didn't. The key question is: what's different?\n\nThe LCD driver changes I made restructured the initialization:\n1. Panel Init → IRQ → LCDC config → `s_lcd_hw_ready = true` → `lcddev_register` → `fb_register`\n\n\n2. The `putrun`/`putarea` functions now block waiting for `s_lcd_hw_ready`\n\nThe intermittent black screen could stem from a few things. The most likely culprit is that `lv_nuttx_init` opens `/dev/lcd0` and starts writing pixels before `s_lcd_hw_ready` is actually set. Even though I moved `lcd_hw_setup_thread_entry`'s code into `lcd_init_thread_entry` before the device registration, there might still be a race condition where the display is opened and written to prematurely.\n\nAnother possibility is that `lcd_hw_setup_thread_entry` was supposed to run at a different priority or timing than `lcd_init_thread_entry`, and consolidating them changed the initialization order. Or the LCD panel itself needs more time to power up before LCDC initialization happens, and `BSP_LCD_PowerUp()` might not be giving it enough time.\n\nThere's also the chance that `find_right_driver()` calls `Init()` as part of driver discovery, and then my code calls it again, potentially putting the panel in a bad state. Looking at `board_lcd_initialize`, I'm checking if the function was already c